From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 16:19:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] handle the host ending the video of a camera

When the application leaves the foreground the host ends the video of a
camera that has a video stream open: it fails the camera's video buffers
under a black, interrupted record and keeps doing so until the guest
closes the camera's last video stream. The HAL passed those buffers on
as failed buffers, and the camera stayed dark for as long as the
application stayed away. What the HAL does about it depends on the
streams of the session.

A session with a stream whose usage carries the video encoder bit
(GRALLOC_USAGE_HW_VIDEO_ENCODER) is over:

- A video buffer that fails while the camera's newest record is black
  and interrupted marks the source as lost (camera_lost, the rule the
  host's wire contract states).
- The sensor latches the loss in the health of the opened session, fails
  the request as a whole with every output buffer returned as an error,
  and tells the framework ERROR_REQUEST for it, and ERROR_DEVICE besides,
  once per session. The framework returns the buffers of a request only
  when it was told that the request failed: a request that ERROR_DEVICE
  alone named would keep its buffers, and the references to their
  streams, until the camera service ends, and the application could not
  open the camera again.
- Each request the session still holds drains in order as ERROR_REQUEST
  with error buffers, and the frame source is not asked again.
- processCaptureRequest, flush and configureStreams answer INTERNAL_ERROR
  from then on (a flush ends what the session holds as failed requests
  first), a close succeeds, and a session opened afterwards is a new
  one. The state belongs to the opened session, not to a frame source or
  a sensor that a reconfiguration replaces.

Any other session is told nothing:

- The frame source closes the camera's video streams at the start of the
  next request, which ends the host's loss, and opens them again with
  the requests after it. The requests that met the loss fail as they do
  for any interruption.
- A loss again right after waits for the next due time (250 ms); nothing
  waits for it.

A failed buffer without that record keeps its old meaning.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
